<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maronites</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Maronite Christians</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Maronites are Eastern Catholic Christians of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> tradition, named after the 5th-century monk Saint Maron and centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a>. Their church entered full communion with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> period. Under Ottoman rule Maronite lords and peasants shared Mount Lebanon with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a>, and conflict between the two communities in 1840 to 1860 reshaped its government; they remain one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>'s main communities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maronites.jpg" alt="Maronites" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Afever · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Lebanon_(1918-1920).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Maronites take their name from Saint Maron, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> monk whose teachings spread through the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>. Christianity spread slowly in the Lebanese highlands, which were still largely pagan in the 5th century. Maron's first disciple, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a> of Cyrrhus, called the Apostle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, set out in 402 to convert the inhabitants of the coast and mountains. In 451 the Maronites followed the Council of Chalcedon, and a massacre of 350 monks of Maron's monastery in 517 is thought to have resulted, though who ordered it, and the event itself, have been debated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the Muslim conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> in 637, Christians of the lowlands began to settle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a>. In 685 John Maron became the first Patriarch of the Maronite Church, which angered the Byzantine emperor and led to persecution. Maronite monks left the Orontes Valley for Mount Lebanon, and by the 10th century they were the majority of the Christians around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a>. They welcomed the First Crusade in 1096, and the church returned to communion with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1180, although the Maronites hold that they were never out of it.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> conquered the region in 1516, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> took the role of protector of the Maronites in 1649. In the 1860 conflict thousands of Maronites were massacred by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a>; some estimates put the Christian dead at about 11,000. Many fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and emigration to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> began. Famine in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> is estimated to have killed one third to one half of the population. After the war the Maronites campaigned successfully for Greater Lebanon, established in 1920 under the French Mandate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Maronite Church is an Eastern Catholic church in full communion with the pope, using the Antiochian Rite, with its patriarch traditionally seated at Bkerke north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>. Classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> remains its liturgical language alongside Arabic. Under the unwritten <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-pact">National Pact</a> the president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> is a Maronite. Maronite communities also live in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and Israel, and the diaspora is estimated at between 7 and 12 million. In the 20th century many Maronite intellectuals favoured a distinct Lebanese identity, some promoting Phoenicianism, while others, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahlil-gibran">Kahlil Gibran</a>, contributed to modern Arab thought.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maronites” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Lebanon's mountains had long been a refuge for religious communities: <b>Maronite Christians</b> in the north, Druze in the south, Shia in the Beqaa and Jabal Amel.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lebanese-republic">Lebanese Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Druze moved east to the Hauran in waves from 1685, and after the battle of Ain Dara in 1711 many Yamani Druze followed, laying the foundation of the Jabal al-Druze, while <b>Maronites</b> became the dominant group in Mount Lebanon.</blockquote><a href="/religion/druze-faith">Druze faith · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Bosnia resisted Istanbul in 1850–1851, and in Mount Lebanon reform led to the Druze–Maronite conflict of 1860, as France claimed to protect the <b>Maronites</b> and Britain the Druze.</blockquote><a href="/movement/tanzimat">Tanzimat · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>After the end of the Mount Lebanon Emirate in 1842 and fighting between Druze and <b>Maronites</b>, the Ottomans and the European powers agreed to divide the mountain along the Beirut-Damascus road.</blockquote><a href="/state/mount-lebanon-double-qaimaqamate">Mount Lebanon Double Qaimaqamate</a></li></ul></section>
